---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'CATEGORY', title: 'The right primary category', text: 'Your primary profile category is Lawyer. Mediation and case types stay secondary, otherwise you drop back in the main search.' },
  { label: 'PAGES', title: 'Case type pages', text: 'A separate page for every area you focus on: process, duration, documents. A three-paragraph list of areas doesn’t show up in search.' },
  { label: 'CONTENT', title: 'Informative articles', text: 'Clear answers to questions like “How long does a divorce case take?”. Professional rules generally treat this as information, and most competitors leave it empty.' },
  { label: 'LANGUAGE', title: 'Language that respects the rules', text: 'We don’t write superiority claims, success rates, promises of winning or special offers. We build every sentence to the limits of the professional rules.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'yasak', label: 'Advertising rules and SEO' },
  { id: 'kategori', label: 'Choosing the profile category' },
  { id: 'arama', label: 'How do clients search?' },
  { id: 'sayfa', label: 'Which pages on the website?' },
  { id: 'yorum', label: 'Google reviews' },
  { id: 'ilerleme', label: 'How do you see progress?' },
  { id: 'sehir', label: 'Work from outside your city' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does SEO work break the advertising rules for lawyers?',
    a: 'On its own, it doesn’t. The rules mainly target misleading claims and improper solicitation; informative content that describes your firm and your practice areas is a different thing. The risk comes from the wording, and we never use risky wording. The rules differ by country and state, so check anything you’re unsure about with your own regulator or bar.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I ask clients for Google reviews?',
    a: 'Yes, we do ask clients for reviews: when each matter is closed, one client at a time. No bulk sends, no incentives such as gifts or discounts, and no ready-made review text; we leave out anything that comes close to an inducement. We don’t quote client reviews on your site and don’t use star-rating markup. When a review comes in, we write a short reply without going into the case. Some US states and other jurisdictions have their own rules on asking clients for reviews, so check yours with your bar or law society.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I list my practice areas on the website?',
    a: 'You can, listing your areas counts as information. Watch out for words that present an area like a title (such as “specialist” where you hold no formal certification) or imply superiority. We write the area names in plain language that makes no claims.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When will I see results?',
    a: 'We can’t give you a timeframe; it depends on where your business starts, on the competition and on the channel. What we can tell you is the order of the work: we measure first, fix the root problems, then produce. You see what we did and how the measurement changed in your weekly report.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="industries/law"
  title="SEO for Lawyers and Google Maps | Wiserrise"
  description="SEO for law firms that respects the advertising rules for lawyers: the right profile category, case type pages and informative content. Weekly report."
  eyebrow="INDUSTRY · LAWYERS"
  h1="SEO for lawyers: become visible by informing, not advertising."
  crumb="Lawyers"
  lede="SEO for lawyers means building your profile and website with informative content so your firm shows up on the map and in the results for searches like “divorce lawyer Manchester”. The advertising rules for lawyers, which differ by country and state, are wary of superiority claims and promises and leave room for information. We run the whole job inside that line."
  serviceName="SEO for Lawyers"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Industries', href: p('industries') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp'), text: 'We set up the category and service structure in language that fits your professional rules.' },
    { label: 'Content Production', href: p('services/content'), text: 'We write case type pages and informative articles around your firm’s practice areas.' },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We fix your profile and your website together for the race on the map.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="yasak">Can you do SEO within the advertising rules for lawyers?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes. Lawyer advertising is regulated almost everywhere, and the rules differ by country and state. In England and
    Wales the SRA sets them, in the US each state has its own rules, many based on the ABA Model Rules 7.1-7.3, and in
    Australia they come from state law and the Legal Profession Uniform Law where it applies.
  </p>

  <p>
    What these rules are wary of is misleading claims, praise and solicitation. Describing who your firm is and which areas it works in counts as
    information, and the work we call SEO makes sure that information can be found on Google. The risk comes from the
    sentences written on the page.
  </p>

  <p>This is where we draw the line:</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>We don’t write</th>
          <th>We write</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>“90% success rate”, number of cases won</td>
          <td>How the case works and how long it takes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>“The best criminal lawyer in the city”</td>
          <td>A plain list of your practice areas</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Client reviews, thank-you letters</td>
          <td>Where you’re admitted, your registration or license number and professional background</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Discounts, special offers, free consultation offers</td>
          <td>Contact details and opening hours</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Comparisons with rival firms</td>
          <td>Your publications and articles</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Law is the most restricted of the <a href={p('industries')}>industries we work in</a>. If you ask us, this restriction
    works in favor of the firm that does its job properly. Some rival firms write risky copy without ever reading the
    rules, and others write nothing at all out of fear.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pages that follow the rules and actually inform are rare in this industry. That gap is open to the firm that wants to
    do its work by the rules.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Nothing on this page is legal advice.</strong> Your own regulator, bar or law society has the final word on
      interpreting the rules. Ask them before publishing any wording you’re unsure about.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="kategori">Which category should you choose on your Google profile?</h2>

  <p>
    The primary category should be Lawyer. It’s the field law firms set up wrong most, and we see the mistake most with
    lawyers who also work as mediators: the primary category is set to Mediator.
  </p>

  <p>
    This choice pushes you down in searches made with the word “lawyer”. The right setup is Lawyer as the primary category
    and mediation as a secondary one. A separate page on the website then handles the demand for mediation.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second common mistake is filling the secondary categories with every case type. Google’s list has specific lawyer
    categories for areas such as criminal and divorce law, and adding all of them blurs the profile. We limit it to the two
    or three areas you really focus on; we explain the details on the
    <a href={p('services/gbp')}>Google Business Profile management</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="arama">How do clients search on Google?</h2>

  <p>They search in two ways. Mixing these two up is the content mistake that costs law firms the most.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Looking for information.</strong> “How long does a divorce take”, “how is severance pay calculated”.
      They’re trying to understand their situation and haven’t started looking for a lawyer yet. These searches rank
      independently of the city.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Looking for a lawyer.</strong> “Divorce lawyer Denver”, “criminal lawyer near me”. They’re about to decide.
      In these searches the map comes first, and proximity to the searcher decides.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    You meet the person looking for a lawyer on the map, and the person looking for information with the articles on your
    website. A firm that only writes blog posts and leaves the profile empty gets traffic, and the phone stays silent. It’s
    the picture we see most in this industry.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sayfa">Which pages should your firm’s website have?</h2>

  <p>
    A separate page for each area you focus on. Most law firm websites follow the same template: a home page, an about page
    and a practice areas page that gives each area three paragraphs. This structure shows up in none of the case type
    searches, because it never really explains any area.
  </p>

  <p>This is the structure we build:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>A separate page for each main practice area: process, duration, required documents, frequently asked questions</li>
    <li>A practice areas page that brings these pages together</li>
    <li>Informative articles that link to the relevant area page</li>
    <li>Lawyer credentials: where you’re admitted, registration or license number, professional background</li>
    <li>LegalService structured data that matches the details on your Google profile exactly</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The credentials page does two jobs. It gathers plain factual information about you in one place, and it shows Google
    that a real, registered lawyer stands behind the site. We write these pages around your firm’s areas as part of
    <a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yorum">What about Google reviews?</h2>

  <p>
    Reviews are a strong signal for the map ranking. In law, they’re also the topic that clashes most with the rules.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are two problems. Asking for reviews with a gift or a ready-made text comes very close to an inducement aimed
    at getting work, and some US states and other jurisdictions have specific rules on asking clients for reviews. In
    criminal and family law, clients don’t want to write about the service they
    received where everyone can see it, and that’s a line to respect.
  </p>

  <p>
    So we ask for reviews in a plain routine: when each matter is closed, one client at a time. No bulk sends, no
    incentives such as gifts or discounts, and no ready-made review text. We don’t quote client reviews on your site and
    don’t use star-rating markup. A complete profile and in-depth area pages carry the rest. When a review arrives, we
    write a short reply without going into the case at all. Replies to 1- and 2-star reviews come to you for approval
    before they go live. Before we start, check the rules on review requests with your own bar or law society; if they
    rule something out, we leave it out.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Client information never becomes content.</strong> In no article do we use case details, information about
      the parties or any detail that could identify a client. We don’t mention the case even when replying to a negative
      review.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ilerleme">How do you see progress?</h2>

  <p>
    First we measure where you rank from different points around your firm for searches like “criminal lawyer”. We can’t
    give you a timeframe, because competition is different in every city and for every case type. What we can tell you is
    the order of the work: we measure first, fix the root problems, then produce. You see what we did and how the
    measurement changed in your weekly report and in the client portal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sehir">Does work come in from outside your city?</h2>

  <p>
    It can, and the way in is the informative articles on your website. The map shows firms close to the searcher, and
    opening a second profile for another city is against Google’s rules.
  </p>

  <p>
    Information searches, on the other hand, rank independently of the city. For consultancy and commercial disputes that
    can be handled remotely, these articles are the most realistic way for a client in another city to find you.
  </p>

  <p>
    We’re careful with city pages. Opening a page for a city where you don’t actually take cases runs into Google’s rule on
    duplicate pages, and it can be risky under professional rules too. So we open them only for places you really work in, with
    content specific to that place.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Which search is your firm <span class="highlight">missing</span> from?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
